---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHero from "../components/PageHero.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Who keeps this archive, what it is for, and why it looks the way it does."
>
  <div class="shell page-shell page-shell--narrow">
    <PageHero
      index="03"
      title="About"
      description="Who keeps this archive, and why it looks like this."
      scene="console"
    />

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I hold an MSc in Bioengineering. Most of what I make is small and
        practical: tools that solve a problem I actually had, then get written
        down properly so the next person does not have to solve it again.
      </p>

      <h2>What I like</h2>
      <p>
        Japanese animation, novels, electronic music, and rhythm games. Those
        look like four separate hobbies, but for me they are mostly one taste
        wearing different clothes.
      </p>
      <p>
        The works I keep returning to hand you a world late. A single concept
        painting, a few bars of music, one unfamiliar word, one line of setting,
        and suddenly the place seems to exist. Nobody stops to explain it. The
        important events have usually already happened, and what is left is
        machinery still running, records nobody is reading any more, and a
        signal with no audience left to receive it. I would rather reconstruct
        something from what outlived it than watch it happen in order.
      </p>
      <p>
        Rhythm games belong to the same taste rather than sitting outside it.
        The appeal is not reflex. It is that a chart is a piece of notation, a
        fixed structure someone designed, which you learn by repetition until
        it stops being a test and becomes a route you know. That is not far
        from why I like the rest of it.
      </p>

      <h2>Why a personal site</h2>
      <p>
        Most of what I build lives in repositories. Repositories are good at
        holding code and bad at holding context: why a thing exists, what it
        was for, what it turned into, what quietly stopped. Platforms are worse,
        because they are built around what is recent rather than what remains.
      </p>
      <p>
        So this is somewhere that keeps things. Finished tools, notes, and the
        unfinished ones too, in a place I control from end to end, that will
        still read the same in ten years. An archive rather than a feed.
      </p>

      <h2>Why it looks like this</h2>
      <p>
        The atmosphere is deliberate, and it follows directly from the taste
        above: a lost or emptied world, machinery that outlived its operators,
        and a certain nostalgia for a future that has already gone out of date.
        The imagery is painted rather than photographed, because an era
        imagining a digital future is more honest as an illustration than as a
        picture of real hardware. The palette sits at dusk. One recording is
        kept in the footer, silent until you ask for it. The small marks beside
        each record are an invented notation, there to suggest that a larger
        index once surrounded these entries.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is still an ordinary website underneath, and that limit is
        intentional. Nothing here is written in character, the notation does not
        translate to anything, and no navigation depends on the mood. If the
        atmosphere ever gets in the way of reading a page or finding a link,
        the atmosphere is what is wrong.
      </p>

      <h2>Elsewhere</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/endoretic">GitHub</a></li>
      </ul>

      <p class="pending-note">[TODO: owner-supplied contact details]</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
